+Attempts to implement neural control models exposed two further gaps. First, the project lacked a structured, searchable resource connecting measured sensory-afferent properties to the species, preparations, and pathway roles on which controller design depends. Those findings were distributed across primary papers and reviews \citep{van_de_crommert_review_1998, windhorst_muscle_2007, windhorst_spinal_2022}, and were difficult for researchers entering the field to locate and compare. Second, the project lacked validated neuromechanical models linking a spinal CPG to both a human-scale musculoskeletal model and its BPA-routed robot counterpart. Published CPG architectures and simulation tools supplied components \citep{rybak_modelling_2006, mccrea_organization_2008, markin_afferent_2010, nourse_sns_2023}, but this project still needed to combine and test them in the human and BPA-routed robot models. The SADb and simulation chapters address these evidence and implementation gaps, respectively.
 
 The dissertation therefore addresses four connected needs: an actuator-force model that accounts for resting length, corrections for transmission losses at the knee, a curated sensory-afferent resource, and tools for developing and checking neuromechanical simulations. Together, they support the design and evaluation of the robot; achieving unsupported walking remains a separate control and integration problem.
 
